Beryllium: a confined renderer keeps video frames out of GPU memory buffers on every driver

Purpose: plan/18 section 1.2 and 1.3 step 4 (feature matrix S-IPC-01,
  S-REN-05); the owner's decision of 11 November 2026: a renderer under the
  lockdown never holds a device descriptor. A GPU memory buffer for a video
  frame is a device descriptor (a buffer object of the graphics driver that
  the renderer maps). The descriptor policy has no kind for it, so the
  sender removes every right before sending it and the renderer's own
  limits do the same on arrival: the frame would not be drawn. On the
  owner's NVIDIA machine this never arises, because
  beryllium-bsd-nvidia-video-frames.patch turns the path off there for
  another reason. Mesa's drivers use it by default.
  - content/renderer/render_thread_impl.cc: a renderer that is in
    capability mode does not use GPU memory buffers for video or camera
    frames, whatever the driver; frames are copied through shared memory
    and uploaded by the GPU process, as with
    --disable-gpu-memory-buffer-video-frames.
    --enable-gpu-memory-buffer-video-frames does not opt a confined
    renderer back in. A renderer that is not confined is unchanged.
  The cost is one copy a frame on Mesa under the lockdown, accepted in the
  decision and not measured: nobody has run the lockdown on a Mesa machine
  (freebsd/tools/gpu-probe says whether a machine would be one).
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on
  beryllium-bsd-nvidia-video-frames.patch (the place in the file) and
  beryllium-descriptor-rights.patch (base/posix/capsicum_rights.h).
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. First
  built in the test build of 0.20.3.22 (16 November 2026); on the owner's
  NVIDIA machine the path was off already, so the run shows only that the
  clips still play.
Test: freebsd/tests/browser/run media --lockdown (the clips play in a
  confined renderer) shows the path works; that it is taken on a Mesa
  driver needs a Mesa machine, where the descriptor policy test would name
  a device descriptor in a renderer if it were not.
security-review: required (which descriptors a confined process is given)

--- a/content/renderer/render_thread_impl.cc
+++ b/content/renderer/render_thread_impl.cc
@@ -217,6 +217,10 @@
 #include "media/mojo/mojom/interface_factory.mojom.h"
 #endif
 
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_FREEBSD)
+#include "base/posix/capsicum_rights.h"
+#endif
+
 #if BUILDFLAG(IS_FUCHSIA)
 #include "media/mojo/clients/mojo_codec_factory_fuchsia.h"
 #include "media/mojo/mojom/fuchsia_media.mojom.h"
@@ -1037,6 +1041,16 @@
       !cmd_line->HasSwitch(switches::kEnableGpuMemoryBufferVideoFrames)) {
     enable_gpu_memory_buffers = false;
   }
+  // Beryllium (plan/18 section 1.2; decided 11 November 2026): a confined
+  // renderer never holds a device descriptor. A GPU memory buffer for a
+  // video frame is one, on every driver that hands them out (Mesa's do by
+  // default), and the policy has no kind for it: the sender would remove
+  // every right and the frame would be lost. So a renderer in capability
+  // mode copies its frames the ordinary way on every driver, and the
+  // switch that opts back in does not apply to it.
+  if (base::freebsd::InCapabilityMode()) {
+    enable_gpu_memory_buffers = false;
+  }
 #endif
   const bool enable_media_stream_gpu_memory_buffers = enable_gpu_memory_buffers;
   bool enable_video_gpu_memory_buffers = enable_gpu_memory_buffers;
